# core/resolve-model - Which model does this phrase refer to?
#
# Answers: candidate models whose title, name, native id, description, source
# filename, status or metamodel matches TERM, with which field matched and how
# closely.
#
# Does NOT prove: that the top-ranked model is the one meant, and never that the
# model is current or authoritative. Rank measures string similarity, nothing else.
#
# core/resolve-element resolves a CONCEPT - an application, a task, a capability.
# This resolves the MODEL that holds concepts. Different populations, and a phrase
# can belong to either: "New Errata Ingestion" turned out to be a BPMN model rather
# than an element, and searching concepts for it returns nothing while reading as
# absence.
#
# The fields a model is findable by are spread across graphs - the model resource
# and its metamodel are semantic, while title, description, source file and status
# are provenance - so the match is unscoped and the model resource is what anchors
# it. ?model is a model because the first block says so; nothing else is trusted.
#
# The model is read through the `model` GRAPH ROLE, not the semantic one, because that
# is where arch:Model and its metamodel conformance live. A profile describing an older
# dataset binds the role to `graph/semantic` instead and this template needs no edit -
# which is the point of asking for a role rather than naming a graph.
#
# dct:title and dct:description are read directly rather than through a role for one
# reason: core/models already reads dct:title directly, and two answers to "what is
# a model called here" would be worse than either alone. The fields that genuinely
# vary by dataset - label, native id, source file, status, metamodel - go through
# roles.
#
# ?matched is numbered by how much a hit is worth, so a title match sorts above a
# match inside a filename, and ?rank orders "1 exact", "2 prefix", "3 contains".
# A plain string ORDER BY then puts the best candidate first, with no scoring
# arithmetic anyone has to trust.
#
# ?status is projected on every row deliberately. A model still under development,
# or one whose conversion did not complete, is exactly the candidate that needs to
# be visible before an answer is built on it.
#
# The two projection OPTIONALs each carry their own triple pattern, and each opens
# an independent graph scope. Both matter. A group of nothing but OPTIONALs matches
# a graph trivially, leaving ?model unbound in that solution, which is compatible
# with every row and multiplies the result once per provenance graph in the dataset -
# measured, not theorised. Independent scopes then allow a store that keeps title and
# status in different provenance graphs, without forcing them into the same one.
#
# Parameters: TERM, LIMIT
{{PREFIXES}}
SELECT ?rank ?matched ?value ?model ?title ?status ?metamodel
WHERE {
  {{GRAPH_OPEN:model}}
    ?model a {{ROLE:model_class}} .
    OPTIONAL { ?model {{ROLE:conforms_to_metamodel}} ?metamodel }
  {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
  OPTIONAL { {{GRAPH_OPEN:provenance}} ?model dct:title ?title {{GRAPH_CLOSE}} }
  OPTIONAL { {{GRAPH_OPEN:provenance2}} ?model {{ROLE:model_status}} ?status {{GRAPH_CLOSE}} }
  {
    {{GRAPH_OPEN:any}}
      VALUES (?field ?matched) {
        (dct:title                      "1 title")
        ({{ROLE:label}}                 "2 name")
        (dct:description                "4 description")
        ({{ROLE:source_file}}           "5 source")
        ({{ROLE:model_status}}          "6 status")
        ({{ROLE:conforms_to_metamodel}} "7 notation")
      }
      ?model ?field ?value .
    {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
  }
  UNION
  {
    {{GRAPH_OPEN:any}}
      ?model {{PATH:native_id}} ?value .
      BIND("3 id" AS ?matched)
    {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
  }
  FILTER(CONTAINS(LCASE(STR(?value)), LCASE({{TERM}})))
  BIND(
    IF(LCASE(STR(?value)) = LCASE({{TERM}}), "1 exact",
      IF(STRSTARTS(LCASE(STR(?value)), LCASE({{TERM}})), "2 prefix", "3 contains")
    ) AS ?rank
  )
}
ORDER BY ?rank ?matched ?model
LIMIT {{LIMIT}}
